and are libertarian in spirit

00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:33.579
or in many policies,
people on the left.

00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:36.516
And I think Bill Maher
is pretty good there

00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:38.484
on the left side of things.

00:08:38.484 --> 00:08:41.387 align:start size:88% position:13%
And his really important
function now is

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:45.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
he is one of the few
people in a kind of major,

00:08:45.258 --> 00:08:46.592
what's now legacy media.

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:48.494
It's funny to think
that HBO is actually

00:08:48.494 --> 00:08:49.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
kind of old school.
- Yeah.

00:08:49.595 --> 00:08:51.564 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah.
- But he was sticking up

00:08:51.564 --> 00:08:54.000
for freedom of speech and
freedom of expression.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, at point in that
show, he was saying like,

00:08:56.369 --> 00:08:59.906 align:start size:88% position:13%
can we hold two thoughts
at the same time that

00:08:59.906 --> 00:09:02.208 align:start size:81% position:19%
for instance, that
we were talking

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:04.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
about Asian violence
against Asians in America,

00:09:04.777 --> 00:09:06.012
and he was like, yeah,

00:09:06.012 --> 00:09:08.447
there could be some of
that coming out of COVID

00:09:08.447 --> 00:09:11.651 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it's also that the
Atlanta shooting was not that.

00:09:11.651 --> 00:09:12.952 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Hmm.
- And I like

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:14.253
to hear that kind of stuff,

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:16.122
from whether it's from
the right or the left,

00:09:16.122 --> 00:09:17.623
like people who are just saying,

00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:21.561
let's have a conversation that
is not simply talking points

00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:25.331 align:start size:97% position:3%
or, slurs like Heidi
Heitkamp was tossing around.

00:09:25.331 --> 00:09:27.366
- So it sounds to me like
you'd kind of love him

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:29.035 align:start size:84% position:16%
to say he was a
libertarian in a way,

00:09:29.035 --> 00:09:30.369 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause it would be great
for libertarianism,

00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:31.337
but on another hand.

00:09:31.337 --> 00:09:32.038 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Huh.
- He serves this sort of

00:09:32.038 --> 00:09:33.039
function.

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:34.540 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah.
- As a remaining liberal

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:35.408
let's say.

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:36.909
- I think so, yeah.

00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:39.545
And, again like right left
libertarians, like some are,

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:42.381
sometimes you hear
conservatorium and
left libertarians

00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:44.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
and whatever, I'm less
interested in that

00:09:44.984 --> 00:09:47.286
and the slogans or the labels

00:09:47.286 --> 00:09:48.688
and I'm more interested in like

00:09:48.688 --> 00:09:51.490 align:start size:97% position:3%
when you see people actually
having conversations

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:54.927
and one of the great virtues
of his show, I think real time

00:09:54.927 --> 00:09:56.963 line:0%
and before that politically
incorrect when it was on ABC

00:09:56.963 --> 00:09:57.863 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
- Hmm.
- And before that

00:09:57.863 --> 00:09:59.131 line:0%
on Comedy Central,

00:09:59.131 --> 00:10:01.667 line:0%
was that it was a
staging ground for

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:03.536
people who disagree
with each other,

00:10:03.536 --> 00:10:06.606
getting to air there,
to have a conversation

00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:10.076
or even an argument for more
than 30 seconds at a time.

00:10:10.076 --> 00:10:11.677 align:start size:84% position:16%
One of the things that
I think you've done

00:10:11.677 --> 00:10:12.912
in a host of other people

00:10:12.912 --> 00:10:15.881 align:start size:88% position:13%
is really take advantage
of the space and time

00:10:15.881 --> 00:10:17.984
that YouTube and
other platforms,

00:10:17.984 --> 00:10:21.287
the Blaze actually is also
a fascinating network to me.

00:10:21.287 --> 00:10:22.755 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Yeah.
- Because it's,

00:10:22.755 --> 00:10:25.191
the one thing you can't say
about the Blaze is that,

00:10:25.191 --> 00:10:29.395
oh, they're only talking
in 15 second sound bites,

00:10:29.395 --> 00:10:32.565
like CNN and MSNBC or Fox even,

00:10:32.565 --> 00:10:34.266
like these are long shows

00:10:34.266 --> 00:10:36.869
that are taking advantage of
the time and space we have.

00:10:36.869 --> 00:10:38.738 align:start size:84% position:16%
So more power to
build more for them.

00:10:38.738 --> 00:10:40.006
- Yeah, all right.

00:10:40.006 --> 00:10:41.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
So let's move off Bill
Maher for a moment.

00:10:41.841 --> 00:10:43.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah.
- I'm gonna keep working,

00:10:43.042 --> 00:10:44.610
I'm gonna keep nudging him

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.845 align:start size:72% position:28%
on my show.
- yeah.

00:10:45.845 --> 00:10:47.513 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That's my plan is just
to keep nudging him,

00:10:47.513 --> 00:10:48.748
'cause it's like the Democrats,

00:10:48.748 --> 00:10:50.282
when they come to cancel him,

00:10:50.282 --> 00:10:53.185
I said this to him once at
an after party at Realtime,

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:54.987
I said, you know, when they
come to get you one day,

00:10:54.987 --> 00:10:56.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's gonna be the
left, not the right.

00:10:56.822 --> 00:10:58.891
- Yeah. I think he
knows that because I,

00:10:58.891 --> 00:11:00.393
and I think it was
during the show,

00:11:00.393 --> 00:11:01.861
like a portion of that era,

00:11:01.861 --> 00:11:04.864 align:start position:0%
because of COVID there were
fewer guests and no after party

00:11:04.864 --> 00:11:06.098
or anything like that,

00:11:06.098 --> 00:11:09.035
but he was saying that
like when he goes places

00:11:09.035 --> 00:11:11.837
like people are often
afraid to speak up

00:11:11.837 --> 00:11:13.806
even in private gatherings

00:11:13.806 --> 00:11:15.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
because if you don't
know everybody,

00:11:15.675 --> 00:11:18.077
I mean, it's kind of like
everybody's in the mafia

00:11:18.077 --> 00:11:20.413 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they're always figuring
there's a couple of

00:11:20.413 --> 00:11:22.481
federal agents who are
recording everything,

00:11:22.481 --> 00:11:25.117 align:start size:97% position:3%
so you don't say anything
if you don't know everybody,

00:11:25.117 --> 00:11:28.020 align:start size:84% position:16%
what a fucked up
world to be living in,

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:32.825
like where we have, some
time since the early 60s,

00:11:32.825 --> 00:11:36.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
we kicked free of all
the dumbest restraints
on free speech,

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:38.864 align:start size:84% position:16%
government censorship,
you could publish,

00:11:38.864 --> 00:11:42.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
you couldn't legally publish
"Lady Chatterley's Lover"

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:43.903
James Joyce's, "Ulysses",

00:11:43.903 --> 00:11:47.373 align:start position:0%
Henry Miller's, "Tropic of
Cancer", Alan Ginsberg's, "Howl"

00:11:47.373 --> 00:11:48.874
before about 1960

00:11:48.874 --> 00:11:50.676
without worrying about
being dragged into court,

00:11:50.676 --> 00:11:52.044
we got rid of all of that

00:11:52.044 --> 00:11:53.379
and then we created the internet

00:11:53.379 --> 00:11:55.581
which is like an
infinite hard drive

00:11:55.581 --> 00:11:57.917
where you can just load
up everything you want.

00:11:57.917 --> 00:12:00.186
And we're arguing now about like

00:12:00.186 --> 00:12:03.622
policing each other speech in
such picky and horrible ways.

00:12:03.622 --> 00:12:05.991
It's tragic really.

00:12:05.991 --> 00:12:07.626
- There's basically
a protection racket

00:12:07.626 --> 00:12:08.828
and nobody knows who's in it.

00:12:08.828 --> 00:12:09.729 align:start size:88% position:13%
So everyone is sort of.
- Yeah.

00:12:09.729 --> 00:12:11.197
- Hostage at some level.

00:12:11.197 --> 00:12:12.732 align:start size:88% position:13%
All right, so let's just
back up for a second,

00:12:12.732 --> 00:12:15.301
because I do sort
of consider you

00:12:15.301 --> 00:12:16.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
because of what
you do with Reason,

00:12:16.502 --> 00:12:17.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you've been
doing this for for a

00:12:17.837 --> 00:12:18.838 align:start size:84% position:16%
couple of decades now.
- Forever.

00:12:18.838 --> 00:12:20.606
- Forever, basically,

00:12:20.606 --> 00:12:23.109
although I am noticing you're
wearing a suede jacket today

00:12:23.109 --> 00:12:24.343
instead of your trademark

00:12:24.343 --> 00:12:25.811
leather jacket.
- Well, you know.

00:12:25.811 --> 00:12:29.882 align:start size:97% position:3%
Yeah, I think of suede leather
as the same thing really.

00:12:29.882 --> 00:12:31.450 align:start size:75% position:25%
(chuckles)
This is a jacket

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:34.120
that my girlfriend got me for
Christmas and I like it a lot,

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.088
and I've been out
in LA for a while,

00:12:36.088 --> 00:12:38.524
I'm usually in New York or DC,

00:12:38.524 --> 00:12:40.793
and it's kind of
a cowboy jacket.

00:12:40.793 --> 00:12:41.861
So what the hell.

00:12:41.861 --> 00:12:43.028
- People know that

00:12:43.028 --> 00:12:44.830 align:start size:88% position:13%
is Nick Gillespie
walking down the street.

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:46.799
but where did this
all start with you?

00:12:46.799 --> 00:12:48.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
This idea of
freedom and Liberty?

00:12:48.667 --> 00:12:49.935 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Yeah.
- Was this

00:12:49.935 --> 00:12:51.137
five-year-old Nick

00:12:51.137 --> 00:12:52.371 align:start size:75% position:25%
(chuckles)
wanting to play

00:12:52.371 --> 00:12:54.106 align:start size:88% position:13%
fairly with his friends.
- Well.

00:12:54.106 --> 00:12:56.142
- And maybe even exchanges.

00:12:56.142 --> 00:12:58.310
- I did go to a Catholic school,

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:01.380
so like you learn
pretty quickly that

00:13:02.581 --> 00:13:04.583
people wanna impose
limits on everything

00:13:04.583 --> 00:13:06.185
and then you kind of like,

00:13:06.185 --> 00:13:08.120 align:start size:84% position:16%
you get tired of
those limits somewhat.

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:11.624
But a lot of it I think is
just kind of dispositional.

00:13:11.624 --> 00:13:15.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think people, some people
are born wanting to be

00:13:15.294 --> 00:13:16.862
able just to do what they want

00:13:16.862 --> 00:13:18.531
as long as they're not
hurting other people,

00:13:18.531 --> 00:13:21.534
which I think is a
kind of basic impulse

00:13:21.534 --> 00:13:24.703 align:start size:97% position:3%
that defines libertarianism
or libertarian ideas.

00:13:24.703 --> 00:13:27.339 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't even like to use
it as a noun anymore,

00:13:27.339 --> 00:13:28.174 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't like to say.
- Hmm-mm.

00:13:28.174 --> 00:13:29.408
- I am a libertarian

00:13:29.408 --> 00:13:31.777 align:start size:84% position:16%
or there is a thing
called libertarianism.

00:13:31.777 --> 00:13:34.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think it's more directional,
I think it's temperamental,

00:13:34.213 --> 00:13:35.681
I think it's,

00:13:35.681 --> 00:13:38.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
do you think that people
should be given more choice,

00:13:38.217 --> 00:13:40.653
more autonomy, more freedom

00:13:40.653 --> 00:13:42.588 align:start size:84% position:16%
in any given situation
as the default?

00:13:42.588 --> 00:13:45.324
Sometimes we override that,
but in general, it's like,

00:13:45.324 --> 00:13:47.126
yeah, let's have more freedom

00:13:47.126 --> 00:13:49.728
and I'm happy that my
life, I was born in 63,

00:13:49.728 --> 00:13:52.832
most of my life
in most big ways,

00:13:52.832 --> 00:13:54.533
we have been getting
more and more free.

00:13:54.533 --> 00:13:57.303
And I think that's great
economically, culturally,

00:13:57.303 --> 00:14:00.172 align:start size:88% position:13%
individually, lifestyle
wise and stuff.

00:14:00.172 --> 00:14:01.440
But a big part of it was

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:02.942
actually my brother
who's older than me,

00:14:02.942 --> 00:14:04.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was born in Brooklyn,
but grew up in New Jersey.

00:14:04.877 --> 00:14:07.713 align:start size:97% position:3%
And my brother went to
college at Rutgers as I did

00:14:07.713 --> 00:14:12.184
and he discovered Reason
Magazine in the bookstore

00:14:12.184 --> 00:14:13.652 align:start size:84% position:16%
and started sending
it home to me,

00:14:13.652 --> 00:14:16.088 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I was like, oh wow, that's
really interesting stuff.

00:14:16.088 --> 00:14:20.059
The way they do analysis
of the outcomes of policies

00:14:20.059 --> 00:14:22.461 align:start size:84% position:16%
as opposed to simply
the intentions.

00:14:22.461 --> 00:14:24.563
And a lot of times
things that are done

00:14:24.563 --> 00:14:25.798
in the name of making people

00:14:25.798 --> 00:14:28.167 align:start size:84% position:16%
more free or more
equal or better off,

00:14:28.167 --> 00:14:29.969
end up having the
opposite result

00:14:29.969 --> 00:14:32.338
and that really spoke to me.

00:14:32.338 --> 00:14:34.940
And basically by the
time I got to college,

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:36.775 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was calling
myself libertarian.

00:14:36.775 --> 00:14:38.043
And then I went to,

00:14:38.043 --> 00:14:39.912
I worked for a while as
a teen magazine editor,

00:14:39.912 --> 00:14:43.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
a music editor, a
music magazine editor

00:14:43.048 --> 00:14:44.850 align:start size:81% position:19%
and you meet a lot
of people there

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:46.819
who are very into
free expression,

00:14:46.819 --> 00:14:48.921
particularly among teen stars.

00:14:48.921 --> 00:14:51.123
You've never met
people who want to

00:14:51.123 --> 00:14:53.626 align:start size:84% position:16%
have no restraints put
on them whatsoever,

00:14:53.626 --> 00:14:54.860 align:start size:72% position:28%
as you know.
- Right.

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:56.729
- As Corey Haim
or Corey Feldman.

00:14:56.729 --> 00:14:57.663
- At least at that time,

00:14:57.663 --> 00:14:58.998
I'm not sure if those magazines.

00:14:58.998 --> 00:14:59.932 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yes.
- Are so into

00:14:59.932 --> 00:15:00.866
free expression these days.

00:15:00.866 --> 00:15:02.234
- No, they are certainly not.

00:15:02.234 --> 00:15:04.103 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- But in any case,

00:15:04.103 --> 00:15:07.706
I went back to grad school for
English and cultural studies,

00:15:07.706 --> 00:15:10.542 align:start size:88% position:13%
literary and cultural
studies in the late 80s,

00:15:10.542 --> 00:15:12.845 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that was the time
when, what we were calling,

00:15:12.845 --> 00:15:14.146
it was political correctness.

00:15:14.146 --> 00:15:15.281
- Hmm.
- Kind of

00:15:15.281 --> 00:15:18.183 align:start size:88% position:13%
what has become
wokeness, woke activism.

00:15:18.183 --> 00:15:20.819
And I realized I
needed an etiology

00:15:20.819 --> 00:15:23.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I wasn't a
conservative aesthetically

00:15:23.222 --> 00:15:24.790
or personally or
temperamentally,

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:26.458 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I certainly
wasn't a progressive,

00:15:26.458 --> 00:15:28.994 align:start size:94% position:6%
because these were people
who really started,

00:15:28.994 --> 00:15:31.931 align:start size:97% position:3%
this was the beginning of
really clamping down on speech

00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:34.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
and of using identity politics
or something like that

00:15:34.733 --> 00:15:37.536
to shut down conversation
rather than open things up,

00:15:37.536 --> 00:15:41.674
which I thought, that's why I
wanted to, go into academia.

00:15:41.674 --> 00:15:45.077
And I took libertarian
thought more seriously

00:15:45.077 --> 00:15:46.545
and that's kind of,

00:15:46.545 --> 00:15:49.081
and then I got a job
working at Reason, so.

00:15:49.081 --> 00:15:50.983
- It's interesting because
you mentioned something about

00:15:50.983 --> 00:15:52.952 align:start size:84% position:16%
sort of how we're
wired or temperament.

00:15:52.952 --> 00:15:55.187
And I do notice that
one of the reasons

00:15:55.187 --> 00:15:57.423
I love going to
libertarian talks

00:15:57.423 --> 00:15:58.791
and events and college things

00:15:58.791 --> 00:16:01.593
and YAF and all of
those things and YAL

00:16:01.593 --> 00:16:03.429
and all those, there's
a lot of Y's in there.

00:16:03.429 --> 00:16:04.763 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- But is that the

00:16:04.763 --> 00:16:07.066
general disposition of people?

00:16:07.066 --> 00:16:10.769 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because the default is I don't
want to control everyone.

00:16:10.769 --> 00:16:12.071 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Right.
- The disposition

00:16:12.071 --> 00:16:13.339
is quite pleasant.

00:16:13.339 --> 00:16:14.807
I mean, there's very rarely,

00:16:14.807 --> 00:16:16.408 align:start size:97% position:3%
can you remember once going
to a libertarian event?

00:16:16.408 --> 00:16:18.811 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, they got the
convention with the naked guy,

00:16:18.811 --> 00:16:21.880 align:start size:88% position:13%
but where it's like a
real fire and brimstone,

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:23.649
like angry people.

00:16:23.649 --> 00:16:25.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that that's
an important piece of this

00:16:25.417 --> 00:16:28.120 align:start size:84% position:16%
which may be just sort
of how we're wired.

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.055
- I think, well, two things,

00:16:30.055 --> 00:16:32.391
one is libertarians believe in

00:16:32.391 --> 00:16:35.427 align:start size:94% position:6%
what sometimes gets called
"the right of exit",

00:16:35.427 --> 00:16:39.631
and so like, if you have
a conference in Las Vegas

00:16:39.631 --> 00:16:42.868
or Washington DC or
Antarctica, whatever

00:16:42.868 --> 00:16:45.037 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then you start screaming
and yelling at people

00:16:45.037 --> 00:16:46.438
that they're gonna go to hell

00:16:46.438 --> 00:16:47.506 align:start size:97% position:3%
or that they're not allowed
to do what you want.

00:16:47.506 --> 00:16:49.274
Like, people leave that room

00:16:49.274 --> 00:16:50.609 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they go out the hallway.
- Right.

00:16:50.609 --> 00:16:51.543
- And start talking.

00:16:51.543 --> 00:16:53.812
So, it works pretty well.

00:16:53.812 --> 00:16:56.115
I think for me one
of the questions is,

00:16:56.115 --> 00:16:57.349
I don't think it's simply,

00:16:57.349 --> 00:16:59.418
you're either born
this way or that way.

00:16:59.418 --> 00:17:01.053
I like Lady Gaga was music,

00:17:01.053 --> 00:17:04.423
and I liked the idea when her
